module

public import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.Curve.Compactification
public import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.AbelianVariety.Jacobian
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.QuotientGroup.Basic

/-!
# The Picard group of an affine curve as a Jacobian quotient

Source: blueprint, sec-curve-estimates/sk-prop-cd-affine/lem-sc-pic-affine,
first proof paragraph (blueprint: lem-sc-pic-affine-quotient).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory Limits MonoidalCategory CartesianMonoidalCategory MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Source: blueprint, sec-curve-estimates/sk-prop-cd-affine/lem-sc-pic-affine,
first proof paragraph (blueprint: lem-sc-pic-affine-quotient).

For every smooth proper connected compactification, its boundary is nonempty. For every
boundary base point, Pic of the affine curve is the quotient of the Jacobian points by the
subgroup generated by boundary point differences. The degree-zero Picard subgroup is `G`,
and `e` is the given identification with Jacobian points. Smoothness of the connected ambient
curve is expressed as smoothness of relative dimension one; geometric integrality follows
from smooth connectedness over the algebraically closed field. Point differences are written
as quotients in the multiplicatively written Picard group.

Proof, step 1 (lem-sc-proper-affine-finite, def-sc-curves): an empty boundary would make the
connected one-dimensional curve both proper and affine, hence finite and zero-dimensional.
Step 2 (lem-sc-divisor-class-curve): restriction of divisors forgets boundary coefficients
and is surjective. Both curves have the same function field, so principal divisors restrict
onto principal divisors. The kernel on divisor classes, hence on Picard groups, is generated
by boundary point classes. Thus Pic(C) is Pic of the compactification modulo this subgroup.
Step 3 (lem-sc-pic-proper-decomposition): the base point has degree one. The retraction
`x ↦ x / [P₀]^(deg x)` onto Pic⁰ has kernel generated by `[P₀]` and maps the boundary subgroup
onto the subgroup generated by `[P]/[P₀]`. It therefore induces an isomorphism of the two
quotients. Step 4 (def-sc-jacobian, thm-sc-jacobian-representability): transport this subgroup
and quotient along the given group identification `e` of Pic⁰ with the Jacobian points. -/
theorem IsConnectedCurve.pic_affine_quotient
    {k : Type u} [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] {C : Scheme.{u}}
    (f : C ⟶ Spec (.of k)) [IsConnectedCurve f] [Smooth f] [IsAffine C]
    (c : Compactification f) [IsConnectedCurve c.g] [Smooth c.g]
    [hsm : SmoothOfRelativeDimension 1 c.g] [GeometricallyIntegral c.g]
    (hfin : (Set.range c.i.base)ᶜ.Finite)
    (hcl : ∀ x ∈ (Set.range c.i.base)ᶜ, IsClosed ({x} : Set c.Z))
    (J : Over (Spec (.of k))) [CommGrpObj J] [IsJacobian c.g J]
    (G : Subgroup c.Z.Pic) (hG : (G : Set c.Z.Pic) = c.g.picZero)
    (e : G ≃* (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of k))) ⟶ J)) :
    (Set.range c.i.base)ᶜ.Nonempty ∧
      ∀ (P₀ : closedPoints c.Z), P₀.val ∉ Set.range c.i.base →
        Nonempty (Additive C.Pic ≃+
          Additive (𝟙_ (Over (Spec (.of k))) ⟶ J) ⧸
            AddSubgroup.closure
              {a | ∃ (P : closedPoints c.Z) (L : G),
                P.val ∉ Set.range c.i.base ∧
                L.val = @Scheme.Pic.ofClosedPoint k _ c.Z ⟨c.g⟩ hsm P /
                  @Scheme.Pic.ofClosedPoint k _ c.Z ⟨c.g⟩ hsm P₀ ∧
                a = Additive.ofMul (e L)}) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
